{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",
  extends: [
    // `github>`, NOT `local>`. They are identical when Renovate runs against
    // GitHub, because `local>` resolves against whatever platform the bot is
    // on -- but this file is now also consumed by the self-hosted Forgejo bot
    // through `globalExtends`
    // (kubernetes/apps/coder/renovate/renovatejob.yaml), and there `local>`
    // resolves to `david-driscoll/.github` ON git.driscoll.tech, which does not
    // exist. The lookup fails the whole preset chain, so every Forgejo repo
    // errors instead of inheriting anything.
    //
    // Renovate reads the platform and endpoint out of GlobalConfig for a
    // `local>` preset (lib/config/presets/local/index.ts), so this is not
    // configurable per-consumer -- the reference itself has to name its source.
    "github>david-driscoll/.github:renovate-config",
    "config:recommended",
    "docker:enableMajor",
    "helpers:pinGitHubActionDigests",
    ":dependencyDashboard",
    ":disableRateLimiting",
    ":semanticCommits",
  ],
  dependencyDashboard: true,
  dependencyDashboardTitle: "Renovate Dashboard 🤖",
  suppressNotifications: [
    "prEditedNotification",
    "prIgnoreNotification",
    // `dependencyLookupWarnings` used to be listed here. It is NOT any more,
    // on purpose: it suppresses EVERY per-package lookup failure, so the
    // dashboard could only ever say "⚠️ WARN: Package lookup failures" without
    // naming one. A permanently unnamed failure cannot be fixed.
    //
    // It was added for "Excess registryUrls found for datasource lookup - using
    // first configured only", which is not caused by overlapping managers: the
    // flux manager resolves a sourceRef by filtering every source in the repo
    // on name + namespace (modules/manager/flux/extract.ts
    // resolveHelmRepository), so duplicate documents put the same URL into
    // registryUrls more than once. The fix is to dedupe the source, and both
    // known duplicates are now gone — stargate-command-cluster#1720 collapsed
    // the two `authentik` HelmRepositories, and `kube-prometheus-stack` moved
    // from three copy-pasted OCIRepository documents into
    // kubernetes/components/repos/kube-prometheus-stack.
    //
    // Verified at the time of removal: 25 HelmRepository, 32 OCIRepository and
    // 2 GitRepository name/namespace pairs, zero duplicated. If the warning
    // returns, look for a new duplicate source rather than re-adding this.
  ],
  // WHAT "Error obtaining docker token" + "Package lookup failures" ACTUALLY
  // MEAN on issue #11, so nobody chases them as a config bug again.
  //
  // They are ONE failure reported twice, and it is infrastructural, not
  // configuration. In lib/modules/datasource/docker/common.ts, the registry
  // token request has explicit branches for 401 (debug, "Unauthorized docker
  // lookup"), 403 (debug, "Not allowed to access docker registry"), 5xx, and
  // -- only when `isDockerHost(registryHost)` -- RequestError and 429, both of
  // which become an ExternalHostError that Renovate treats as retryable and
  // does NOT count as a package-lookup failure. Every other outcome, including
  // a 429 FROM A NON-DOCKER-HUB REGISTRY, falls through to
  //
  //   logger.warn({...}, 'Error obtaining docker token'); return null;
  //
  // A null there makes getTags() null, which makes getReleases() null, which
  // `Result.wrapNullable(fetchCachedReleases(config), 'no-result')` in
  // lib/modules/datasource/index.ts turns into precisely the dashboard's
  // `Failed to look up docker package <name>: no-result`. So the two warnings
  // are the head and tail of one code path: anonymous registry lookups being
  // rate-limited from the Mend-hosted app's shared egress IPs.
  //
  // Three things establish it is rate limiting rather than anything in here:
  //   * all four named packages resolve anonymously from a workstation --
  //     token endpoint 200, tags/list 200, current tag present;
  //   * the failing set ROTATES between runs, and even within one run is
  //     inconsistent per host: `data.forgejo.org/forgejo/runner` looked up
  //     fine (PR #1399) while `data.forgejo.org/forgejo/forgejo` and
  //     `data.forgejo.org/oci/docker` failed on that same host;
  //   * checked against the registries directly, NONE of the four are stale --
  //     gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor v0.55.1, data.forgejo.org/oci/docker 29-dind,
  //     data.forgejo.org/forgejo/forgejo 16.0.3-rootless and
  //     ghcr.io/rajsinghtech/charts/garage-operator 0.7.7 are each the newest
  //     tag on their track. The warning costs visibility, not freshness.
  //
  // This is the docker-datasource twin of the GITHUB_COM_TOKEN note in
  // kubernetes/apps/coder/renovate/renovatejob.yaml: unauthenticated lookups
  // exhaust a per-IP budget and report `no-result` for healthy dependencies.
  // The same remedy applies where a registry accepts credentials -- Mend Cloud
  // no longer reads `encrypted` blocks from the repo, so a token goes in
  // Credentials at developer.mend.io and is referenced as
  // `{{ secrets.NAME }}` from a hostRule. Worth doing for docker.io and
  // ghcr.io; gcr.io and data.forgejo.org serve these repositories
  // anonymously and have nothing to authenticate WITH, so those two stay
  // best-effort and the self-hosted nightly bot (its own egress IP) is the
  // backstop for them.
  //
  // Do NOT "fix" this by adding `dependencyLookupWarnings` back above -- see
  // that comment for why an unnamed failure is worse than a named one.

  // THE MITIGATION for the above, and the reason it is per-host rather than
  // global.
  //
  // The 2026-09-04 evening dashboard broke the original "it is a docker
  // registry 429" reading, and it is worth recording why. That run failed on
  //
  //   gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor, data.forgejo.org/oci/docker,
  //   data.forgejo.org/forgejo-helm/forgejo   -- docker datasource
  //   ZiggyCreatures.FusionCache.Serialization.SystemTextJson, TMDbLib
  //                                           -- NUGET datasource
  //
  // The nuget failures cannot be a docker token problem: different datasource,
  // different code path, no registry token involved. Whatever is happening is
  // therefore NOT specific to container registries, which rules out the
  // getAuthHeaders fall-through as the whole story.
  //
  // What survives the evidence is per-host request pressure. Measured against
  // this repo with `renovate --platform=local --dry-run=lookup` on 44.61.6:
  // 1472 dependencies across 944 files, and ALL 1472 resolved -- zero lookup
  // failures, including all five the hosted run had just failed on, at their
  // correct current values (cadvisor v0.55.1, oci/docker 29-dind,
  // forgejo-helm/forgejo 17.1.5, both nuget packages). 60 hand-rolled requests
  // to the three hosts in a tight loop also returned 200/200. So the packages,
  // the manifests and this config are all fine; what differs is the runner.
  //
  // Renovate ships NO per-host throttling by default -- `concurrentRequestLimit`
  // defaults to null and `maxRequestsPerSecond` to 0, i.e. unlimited. Against a
  // 1472-dependency repo that means large bursts at a handful of hosts from
  // Mend's shared egress IPs, which is exactly the condition under which those
  // hosts shed load. It also explains the two properties that make this so
  // annoying to chase: the failing set ROTATES between runs, and it spans
  // datasources.
  //
  // These are the four hosts observed failing across three dashboard runs.
  // Limits are deliberately modest rather than aggressive: the run is weekly,
  // so wall-clock cost is irrelevant, but strangling ghcr.io would hurt because
  // most of the 1472 live there -- hence its more generous allowance.
  //
  // HONEST CAVEAT: this is unverifiable from here. The failure does not
  // reproduce locally, so this cannot be tested before merge -- it is a
  // targeted, documented, credential-free mitigation, not a proven fix. The
  // success criterion is the "Package lookup failures" warning disappearing
  // from issue #11 over the next few runs. If it does NOT, revert this rather
  // than tightening the numbers further, and treat the residual as Mend-side.
  hostRules: [
    {
      matchHost: "gcr.io",
      concurrentRequestLimit: 2,
      maxRequestsPerSecond: 4,
    },
    {
      matchHost: "data.forgejo.org",
      concurrentRequestLimit: 2,
      maxRequestsPerSecond: 4,
    },
    {
      matchHost: "api.nuget.org",
      concurrentRequestLimit: 4,
      maxRequestsPerSecond: 8,
    },
    {
      // Most of this repo's dependencies resolve here, so this one is a brake,
      // not a throttle.
      matchHost: "ghcr.io",
      concurrentRequestLimit: 8,
      maxRequestsPerSecond: 12,
    },
  ],
  schedule: [
    "every weekend"
  ],
  ignorePaths: [
    "**/*.sops.*"
  ],
  // Off entirely. The shared preset (github>david-driscoll/.github:renovate-config)
  // points the cake manager at `.cs` -- a copy-paste of the `nuget` block right
  // above it there. Cake scripts are `.cake`; a C# file-based app is not one.
  // `managerFilePatterns` is ADDITIVE (verified: renovate 44.48.1 logs both
  // `/\.cake$/` and `/\.cs$/` for manager cake), so it cannot be narrowed from
  // here -- only the manager as a whole can be switched off, and there are no
  // `.cake` files in this repo to lose.
  //
  // Cost of leaving it on: 7 package files, 0 deps. The cake extractor only
  // understands `#addin`/`#tool nuget:?package=...`, never `#:package Foo@1.2.3`,
  // so every one of those files parses to nothing -- while still being listed
  // under "cake (7)" on the dashboard and pushing the real dependencies into
  // the truncated remainder. The `#:package` directives ARE picked up, by the
  // nuget manager (extract/single-csharp-file.ts), which the same preset
  // correctly points at `.cs`.
  cake: {
    enabled: false,
  },
  flux: {
    managerFilePatterns: [
      "/(^|/)kubernetes/.+\\.ya?ml$/"
    ],
    pinDigests: false,
  },
  "helm-values": {
    managerFilePatterns: [
      "/(^|/)kubernetes/.+\\.ya?ml$/"
    ],
    pinDigests: true,
  },
  helmfile: {
    managerFilePatterns: [
      "/(^|/)helmfile\\.ya?ml$/"
    ],
    // WARNING: this line does nothing on its own. Manager-level config is
    // merged before packageRules (workers/repository/process/fetch.ts calls
    // applyPackageRules at 'pre-lookup' *after* the manager config), so the
    // shared preset's `docker:pinDigests` rule
    // ({ matchDatasources: ["docker"], pinDigests: true }) overrides it, and
    // helmfile's oci:// charts use the docker datasource. The effective
    // suppression is a packageRule in local>david-driscoll/.github:renovate-config
    // ({ matchManagers: ["helmfile"], pinDigests: false }). Kept here as
    // documentation of intent — do not rely on it alone.
    pinDigests: false,
  },
  kubernetes: {
    managerFilePatterns: [
      "/(^|/)kubernetes/.+\\.ya?ml$/"
    ],
    pinDigests: true,
  },
  kustomize: {
    managerFilePatterns: [
      "/(^|/)kustomization\\.ya?ml$/"
    ],
    pinDigests: true,
  },
  customDatasources: {
    "1password-cli": {
      defaultRegistryUrlTemplate: "https://app-updates.agilebits.com/product_history/CLI2",
      format: "html",
      // Renovate html format extracts all <a href> values as {version: href}.
      // Filter to darwin_arm64 downloads (one per release), extract version string.
      // Beta URLs contain "beta" in path; mark those isStable: false.
      transformTemplates: [
        '{"releases": releases[$contains(version, "/op_darwin_arm64_v") and $contains(version, ".zip")].{"version": $replace(version, /^.*\\/op_darwin_arm64_(v[0-9.]+(?:-[a-zA-Z0-9.]+)?)\\.zip$/, "$1"), "isStable": $not($contains(version, "beta"))}, "sourceUrl": "https://app-updates.agilebits.com/product_history/CLI2", "homepage": "https://1password.com/downloads/command-line/"}',
      ],
    },
  },
  packageRules: [
    {
      // Jellyfin's 12.0 release candidates are tagged `12.0-rcN`, and the
      // DEFAULT `docker` versioning reads everything after the first `-` as an
      // image VARIANT -- the same slot that holds `alpine` or `rootless` --
      // never as a prerelease. `isCompatible` then demands the suffix match
      // exactly, so `12.0-rc3` is only ever comparable with itself: rc4..rc7
      // each carry a different suffix and are filtered out before sorting.
      //
      // Verified on renovate 44.61.6 against this repo's config: currentVersion
      // parses as bare `12.0`, and the sole update generated is a pinDigest
      // back onto `12.0-rc3` -- which is exactly what issue #11 shows,
      //
      //   `jellyfin/jellyfin 12.0-rc3` -> [Updates: `12.0-rc3`]
      //
      // and why that pin sat five release candidates behind while looking, on
      // the dashboard, like a dependency Renovate was tracking.
      //
      // `loose` is NOT the fix, and was measured rather than assumed: it does
      // produce an update, but to `12.0-rc7.20260831-232051` -- the datestamped
      // per-build tag, which sorts above the clean one. Only a regex versioning
      // that refuses to MATCH those tags (they are excluded as invalid) lands
      // on `12.0-rc7`.
      //
      // The rc number goes in `prerelease`, not `build`, and that choice is
      // what makes this survive GA. `prerelease` marks the tag unstable, which
      // buys two things: `ignoreUnstable` still admits it, because the current
      // value is itself unstable and shares major/minor/patch (filter.ts), and
      // a future stable `12.0.0` outranks every rc, so this pin walks onto the
      // GA release on its own. `build` would instead be appended to the release
      // array, where `12.0.0` -> [12,0,0,0] sorts BELOW `12.0-rc7` ->
      // [12,0,0,7] and the pin could never leave the rc track.
      //
      // Known edge, not worth more machinery: prereleases compare with
      // localeCompare (generic.ts), so `rc10` would sort below `rc9`. Jellyfin
      // has never shipped a double-digit rc; if it ever does, move the tag by
      // hand that one time.
      description: "Jellyfin RC tags: -rcN is a prerelease, not an image variant",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "jellyfin/jellyfin",
        "ghcr.io/jellyfin/jellyfin",
      ],
      versioning: "regex:^(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)(\\.(?<patch>\\d+))?(-rc(?<prerelease>\\d+))?$",
    },
    {
      // Same trap as the jellyfin rule above, different shape of tag. RomM is
      // pinned to `5.1.0-beta.3`, so `docker` versioning reads the variant slot
      // as `beta.3` and will only ever compare it with another `beta.3` -- and
      // the ONLY other one is `5.1.0-beta.3-slim`, whose suffix differs again.
      // The dashboard is unambiguous about the result: the romm entry carries
      // no `-> [Updates: ...]` at all, not even a digest refresh.
      //
      // Meanwhile upstream shipped stable `5.1.0` AND `5.2.0`. A tag with no
      // suffix is not "compatible" with one that has `beta.3`, so a plain
      // release is exactly as invisible here as a newer beta -- this pin cannot
      // walk off the beta channel on its own, which is the part that matters.
      //
      // `semver` rather than a regex, because unlike jellyfin's two-part
      // `12.0-rcN` these tags are already valid semver. Measured on renovate
      // 44.61.6 against this config: `5.1.0-beta.3` -> `5.2.0`, updateType
      // minor, branch renovate/ghcr.io-rommapp-romm-5.x. The `-slim` tags parse
      // as prereleases (`beta.3-slim`) and are dropped by ignoreUnstable, which
      // admits an unstable candidate only when it shares major/minor/patch with
      // the current one -- so the variant does not leak in.
      //
      // Read the release notes before merging what this generates. RomM owns a
      // Postgres schema and this estate has been bitten by its migrations
      // before; a beta-to-stable move across a minor is a migration, not a tag
      // change.
      description: "RomM beta tags: -beta.N is a prerelease, not an image variant",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/rommapp/romm"
      ],
      versioning: "semver",
    },
    {
      description: "CoreDNS Group",
      groupName: "CoreDNS",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/coredns/"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Flux Operator Group",
      groupName: "Flux Operator",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/flux-operator/",
        "/flux-instance/"
      ],
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Siderolabs Talos",
      groupName: "Siderolabs Talos",
      matchDepNames: [
        "ghcr.io/siderolabs/installer",
        "ghcr.io/siderolabs/talosctl",
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Siderolabs Kubernetes",
      groupName: "Siderolabs Kubernetes",
      matchDepNames: [
        "ghcr.io/siderolabs/kubelet"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Tailscale",
      groupName: "Tailscale",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/tailscale/*",
        "tailscale/*",
        "tailscale*"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Traefik",
      groupName: "Traefik",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/traefik/*",
        "traefik/*",
        "traefik*"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Immich",
      groupName: "Immich",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/immich-app/*"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "CloudNative-PG Cluster",
      groupName: "CloudNative-PG Cluster",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgresql"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      // A Postgres major is not an image bump: PGDATA is bound to the major that
      // created it, so crossing one is a pg_upgrade or a dump/restore, never a
      // tag change. Postgres 18 additionally moved its data directory layout and
      // refuses to start when the mount IS the data directory.
      //
      // Both docker/_common/postgres/compose.yaml and the openbao-replica restore
      // test carried comments asserting this rule was enforced here. It was not —
      // docker.io/library/postgres matched no rule at all, and an unguarded major
      // silently broke the OpenBao break-glass restore drill, which nothing
      // noticed because that drill only runs on the 1st of the month.
      //
      // Second correction, this one to the fix itself: the rule that landed here
      // was a verbatim copy of the grouping rule above — same description, same
      // groupName, same body, no `matchUpdateTypes`/`enabled`. So the comment
      // described a guard that still did not exist, in a block that looked like
      // it implemented one. This is that guard, for real.
      //
      // `enabled: false` DROPS the update rather than parking it on the
      // dashboard (see the pinDigest rule below for why: matchUpdateTypes is a
      // post-lookup matcher, so the update is generated and then filtered out).
      // That is deliberate — a Postgres major has to be driven by a written
      // migration, so there is nothing useful for a dashboard entry to offer.
      //
      // Both spellings of the library image are listed because the depName is
      // whatever the manifest wrote: the compose files say
      // `docker.io/library/postgres`, but a bare `postgres:` in any future
      // Dockerfile or compose entry would extract as `postgres`.
      description: "Never propose a Postgres major — PGDATA is bound to it",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgresql",
        "docker.io/library/postgres",
        "postgres"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "major"
      ],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Spegel Group",
      groupName: "Spegel",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/spegel/"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
    },
    {
      description: "Auto-merge GitHub Actions",
      matchManagers: [
        "github-actions"
      ],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "minor",
        "patch",
        "digest"
      ],
      minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
      ignoreTests: true,
    },
    {
      // Flux OCIRepository Helm charts are cosign-verified and tag-pinned; the
      // flux manager owns these files and should never write a digest. The
      // shared preset's docker:pinDigests otherwise generates pinDigest
      // updates for them. helm-values/kubernetes also scan kubernetes/**.yaml
      // with pinDigests: true and race flux to patch the same OCI chart ref.
      // Excluding all three managers here stops both.
      //
      // Note this rule does NOT cover the helmfile manager, which is why the
      // four "pin image ..." entries for coredns / spegel / cert-manager /
      // flux-operator survived it and sat on the dashboard indefinitely. That
      // is fixed in the shared preset, not here — see vault#104 and
      // david-driscoll/.github#2.
      //
      // matchUpdateTypes is a post-lookup matcher (updateType does not exist
      // pre-lookup), so this rule does not prevent the lookup; the update is
      // generated and then dropped by the `enabled !== false` filter in
      // workers/repository/updates/flatten.ts. That is fine here, but it is
      // why the helmfile fix uses `pinDigests: false` + matchManagers instead:
      // that form is evaluated pre-lookup and suppresses generation outright.
      description: "Don't pin digests on flux-managed OCI Helm charts",
      matchManagers: [
        "flux",
        "helm-values",
        "kubernetes"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "pinDigest"
      ],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      // The FOURTH manager that must not be handed a pinDigest, and the one
      // the rule above missed. Every annotation this repo writes is served by
      // the `Process annotated dependencies` customManager at the bottom of
      // this file, and its "full docker image reference" pattern captures
      //
      //   (?<currentValue>[^\s@]+)
      //
      // -- a character class that STOPS AT THE `@`. So `currentValue` is the
      // bare tag, `currentDigest` is never set, and `replaceString` spans only
      // the tag. Renovate therefore believes the dep is unpinned, generates a
      // pinDigest update, and then cannot write it back: doAutoReplace has
      // nowhere to put the `@sha256:` suffix and throws
      // WORKER_FILE_UPDATE_FAILED. That surfaces on the dashboard as
      //
      //   ⚠️ WARN: Error updating branch: update failure
      //
      // with the branch parked under "Errored ... will be retried" forever,
      // because the retry re-runs the same impossible edit. The same note is
      // already written from the other end in
      // kubernetes/apps/coder/renovate/renovatejob.yaml ("No digest, on
      // purpose ... cannot round-trip a `@sha256:` suffix") -- this is that
      // observation turned into a guard.
      //
      // Measured on 44.61.6 against this repo, flattened updates 88 -> 40:
      //
      //   data.forgejo.org/forgejo/forgejo    2 -> 0
      //   ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgresql   4 -> 0
      //   ghcr.io/pulumi/pulumi-nodejs       18 -> 0
      //   ghcr.io/windmill-labs/windmill-extra 2 -> 0
      //   jdxcode/mise                       18 -> 0
      //   minio/mc                            2 -> 0
      //   ghcr.io/renovatebot/renovate        6 -> 4
      //
      // -- exactly the two branches the dashboard had errored, and nothing
      // else. The renovatebot/renovate entry is the proof that this is scoped
      // correctly: it loses its two pinDigest updates and KEEPS its four real
      // version bumps.
      //
      // `custom.regex`, not `regex`. ManagersMatcher (util/package-rules/managers.ts)
      // tests `custom.${manager}` when isCustomManager(manager), so the bare
      // name silently matches nothing -- verified by running both.
      //
      // This is a post-lookup matcher like the rule above, so the update is
      // still generated and then dropped in flatten.ts. `pinDigests: false`
      // would suppress it pre-lookup and was tried first; it does NOT work
      // from a packageRule here, also verified by running it.
      description: "Don't pin digests on annotated deps — the regex can't hold one",
      matchManagers: [
        "custom.regex"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "pinDigest"
      ],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      // bootstrap/helmfile.yaml mirrors chart versions that are otherwise
      // owned by the flux manager (kubernetes/apps/**/helmrelease.yaml).
      // Renovate treats same-named deps found by different managers as one
      // logical update and tries to combine both files' edits into a single
      // branch. Force helmfile-manager updates onto their own branch
      // namespace so they never share a branch with the flux-managed update
      // for the same chart.
      //
      // Correction: the "Error updating branch: update failure" warning that
      // originally motivated this rule was NOT a rebase race. It was the
      // helmfile manager being handed pinDigest updates it cannot apply —
      // helmfile's extractor never sets currentDigest and the manager exports
      // no updateDependency/autoReplaceStringTemplate, so doAutoReplace always
      // throws WORKER_FILE_UPDATE_FAILED. Fixed in the shared preset
      // (david-driscoll/.github#2). This rule is kept because separate branch
      // namespaces are still the right shape for a mirrored chart version.
      description: "Keep bootstrap helmfile chart bumps off the flux-managed branch",
      matchManagers: [
        "helmfile"
      ],
      additionalBranchPrefix: "helmfile-",
    },
    {
      // Talos/Kubernetes upgrade manifests carry a plain `version: vX.Y.Z`
      // field (TalosUpgrade/KubernetesUpgrade) extracted via the custom regex
      // manager. A digest does not belong in that field, so suppress the
      // docker:pinDigests-driven pinDigest updates for these images.
      description: "Don't pin digests on siderolabs upgrade image versions",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchDepNames: [
        "ghcr.io/siderolabs/installer",
        "ghcr.io/siderolabs/kubelet",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "pinDigest"
      ],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "major"
      ],
      semanticCommitType: "feat",
      commitMessagePrefix: "{{semanticCommitType}}({{semanticCommitScope}})!:",
      commitMessageExtra: "( {{currentVersion}} → {{newVersion}} )",
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "minor"
      ],
      semanticCommitType: "feat",
      commitMessageExtra: "( {{currentVersion}} → {{newVersion}} )",
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch"
      ],
      semanticCommitType: "fix",
      commitMessageExtra: "( {{currentVersion}} → {{newVersion}} )",
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "digest"
      ],
      semanticCommitType: "chore",
      commitMessageExtra: "( {{currentDigestShort}} → {{newDigestShort}} )",
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "container",
      commitMessageTopic: "image {{depName}}",
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "helm"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "helm",
      commitMessageTopic: "chart {{depName}}",
    },
    {
      matchManagers: [
        "github-actions"
      ],
      semanticCommitType: "ci",
      semanticCommitScope: "github-action",
      commitMessageTopic: "action {{depName}}",
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "github-releases"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "github-release",
      commitMessageTopic: "release {{depName}}",
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "major"
      ],
      labels: [
        "type/major"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "minor"
      ],
      labels: [
        "type/minor"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch"
      ],
      labels: [
        "type/patch"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/container"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "helm"
      ],
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/helm"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchManagers: [
        "github-actions"
      ],
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/github-action"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "github-releases"
      ],
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/github-release"
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "Override 1password-cli datasource: aqua type:http uses AgileBits CDN, no GitHub releases exist",
      matchManagers: [
        "mise"
      ],
      matchDepNames: [
        "1password-cli"
      ],
      overrideDatasource: "custom.1password-cli",
    },
  ],
  customManagers: [
    {
      // The root Pulumi.yaml `packages:` block is the real pin for every
      // bridged Terraform provider in sdks/. `pulumi package add` writes the
      // version into THREE places -- this file, the SDK's package.json
      // `version`, and a base64 blob at `pulumi.parameterization.value` -- and
      // only this one is human-editable, so it is the only one worth matching.
      //
      // Which means a Renovate bump here is HALF a change, and the other half
      // is SILENT. The base64 blob is what the bridge plugin actually resolves
      // the provider from, and no regex can re-encode it -- so a merged bump
      // that has not been regenerated leaves the SDK bound to the old provider
      // while the diff reads like an upgrade. Nothing fails; `pulumi up` simply
      // keeps running the old version.
      //
      // Regenerate before merging one of these:
      //
      //   pulumi package add terraform-provider <registry-url> <new-version>
      //
      // which rewrites sdks/<name>/ and the entry here. `pulumi package add`
      // reformats `parameters` as a block sequence; put it back to the flow
      // style the other entries use so this manager keeps matching it.
      //
      // Nothing tracked these versions before -- all six providers were pinned
      // by hand and updated only when someone noticed.
      description: "Pulumi bridged Terraform providers (root Pulumi.yaml)",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/)Pulumi\\.ya?ml$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "parameters:\\s*\\[\\s*(?<registryHost>registry\\.(?:terraform\\.io|opentofu\\.org))/(?<depName>[^,\\]\\s]+)\\s*,\\s*(?<currentValue>[^,\\]\\s]+)\\s*\\]",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "terraform-provider",
      // registry.opentofu.org serves the same /v1/providers API, so terrifi
      // resolves through the identical datasource with only the host swapped.
      registryUrlTemplate: "https://{{{registryHost}}}",
    },
    {
      description: "Process annotated dependencies",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/).+\\.env$/",
        "/(^|/).+\\.sh$/",
        "/(^|/).+\\.ya?ml$/",
      ],
      // `matchStringsStrategy` defaults to "any", and "any" does NOT mean
      // "the first pattern that matches". Every matchString is run over the
      // whole file and EVERY match becomes its own dependency
      // (lib/modules/manager/custom/regex/strategies.ts, handleAny), so these
      // four patterns have to be mutually exclusive or one annotation yields
      // two deps.
      //
      // They were not. The bare-value pattern used to end in
      // `(?<currentValue>\\S+)`, which swallowed the two shapes patterns 3 and
      // 4 exist to parse properly: a quoted scalar (currentValue `"18.6"`,
      // quotes and all) and a whole image reference (currentValue
      // `ghcr.io/pulumi/pulumi-nodejs:3.259.0`). Each such annotation produced
      // the correct dep AND a junk twin whose currentValue is not a version.
      // Measured on renovate 44.48.1 against this repo: 71 deps from this
      // manager, of which 24 were junk twins of the other 47 --
      //
      //   data.forgejo.org/forgejo/forgejo, ghcr.io/renovatebot/renovate,
      //   ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgresql (x2), ghcr.io/windmill-labs/windmill-extra,
      //   ghcr.io/pulumi/pulumi-nodejs (x8), jdxcode/mise (x8), minio/mc
      //
      // What that cost, verified rather than assumed: 94 malformed registry
      // requests per run, of the form
      //
      //   HEAD https://ghcr.io/v2/pulumi/pulumi-nodejs/manifests/ghcr.io/pulumi/pulumi-nodejs:3.259.0
      //
      // -- the whole reference pasted in where the tag goes. They all 404,
      // including three against data.forgejo.org, which the Mend-hosted bot is
      // separately failing to reach at the network level (issue #11: "Error
      // obtaining docker token" plus a no-result on all three of its images,
      // none of which reproduce locally).
      //
      // What it did NOT cost, so nobody re-derives it the hard way: no extra
      // PRs. A junk twin carries the same depName and the same pinDigest
      // target as its good sibling, so branchify collapses the two -- the
      // branch set is byte-identical before and after this fix (162 branch
      // entries, 77 of them renovate/pin-dependencies). The standing
      // "pin dependencies" dashboard item is `docker:pinDigests` doing its job
      // on the real deps, not this bug.
      //
      // The reason to fix it anyway is that which twin wins the branch is
      // arbitrary, and the loser's `replaceString` spans the wrong text. A
      // pinDigest applied through the postgres twin rewrites `postgresql:
      // "18.6"` by substituting `"18.6"` INCLUDING the quotes.
      //
      // The fix is the character class: a bare value may contain neither `/`
      // nor `"`, and must be followed by whitespace or end of input. Two
      // things constrain how that trailing condition can be written. Renovate
      // compiles these with RE2, which has no lookahead at all -- a `(?=...)`
      // here does not merely misbehave, it fails config validation and takes
      // the whole run down with "Invalid regExp for customManagers". And the
      // custom manager passes the 'g' flag but NOT 'm' (strategies.ts), so a
      // bare `$` means end of FILE, not end of line. `(?:\\s|$)` satisfies
      // both; it consumes the newline into `replaceString`, which is harmless.
      matchStrings: [
        // Bare values: KEY=v1.2.3 / key: v1.2.3 / key: &anchor 1.2.3
        "datasource=(?<datasource>\\S+) depName=(?<depName>\\S+)( repository=(?<registryUrl>\\S+))?\\n.+(:\\s|=)(&\\S+\\s)?(?<currentValue>[^\\s/\"]+)(?:\\s|$)",
        // Versioned path segment inside a URL: .../v1.2.3/...
        "datasource=(?<datasource>\\S+) depName=(?<depName>\\S+)\\n.+/(?<currentValue>(v|\\d)[^/]+)",
        // Quoted YAML string values: key: "1.2.3"
        "datasource=(?<datasource>\\S+) depName=(?<depName>\\S+)( repository=(?<registryUrl>\\S+))?\\n[^:]+:\\s\"(?<currentValue>[^\"]+)\"",
        // Full docker image references: key: registry/image:tag
        "datasource=(?<datasource>\\S+) depName=(?<depName>\\S+)( repository=(?<registryUrl>\\S+))?\\n[^:\\n]+:\\s[^:\\n]+:(?<currentValue>[^\\s@]+)",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "{{#if datasource}}{{{datasource}}}{{else}}github-releases{{/if}}",
    },
  ],
}
